window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-4efcc7a3b951adb1"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"iterate-on-difficult-problems\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"_1\">迭代解决困难问题</h1> <p>让 Codex 通过有评分的改进循环解决难题。</p> <p>为 Codex 提供评估系统，例如评分脚本和可供审查的产物，让它持续改进困难任务，直到评分达到要求。</p> <p><a id=\"best-for\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_2\">适合哪些情况</h2> <ul> <li>每轮迭代都能评分，但通常需要多轮才能得到最佳结果的问题</li> <li>具有视觉或主观输出，同时需要确定性检查和大模型评审评分的任务</li> <li>希望清楚记录进展、而不是只依赖上下文的长期 Codex 对话</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"starter-prompt\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_3\">起步提示词</h2> <p>持续迭代，直到评估通过</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">这个工作区里有一项困难任务，我希望你通过评估驱动的改进循环来完成它。\n\n改动前：\n- 阅读 `AGENTS.md`。\n- 找到为当前输出评分的脚本或命令。\n\n迭代循环：\n- 每次只做一项有针对性的改进。\n- 每次实质变化后重新运行评估命令。\n- 记录评分以及所作的更改。\n- 直接检查生成的产物。如果输出是视觉内容，请使用 `view_image`。\n- 持续推进，直到总评分和大模型平均评分都超过 90%。\n\n约束：\n- 不要在首次得到尚可的结果时就停止。\n- 除非新版的评分或产物明显更差，否则不要退回旧版。\n- 如果评估有所改善但仍未达标，请说明瓶颈并继续。\n\n输出：\n- 当前最佳评分\n- 主要迭代记录\n- 剩余风险或薄弱环节\n</code></pre> <p><a id=\"related-links\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_4\">关联文档</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">使用 AGENTS.md 设置自定义指令</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-0310706ee8617b64\">Codex 工作流</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。在文档页面 URL 末尾添加 <code>.md</code>，即可获取该页的 Markdown 版本。</p> </blockquote> <p><a id=\"introduction\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_5\">简介</h2> <p>有些任务可以一次验证：构建成功、测试通过，工作就完成了。但有些优化问题很难解决，需要在紧密的评估循环中反复迭代。要判断下一步方向，Codex 需要检查当前输出、评分、决定下一项改动，然后重复这个过程，直到结果真正达到要求。</p> <p>这类用例很适合搭配自定义界面：让 Codex 记录每轮的输出和生成产物，就能直观查看进度。 你可以在应用中看着 Codex 持续工作，而目标产物、模型输出或生成的素材不断改进。 关键是为 Codex 提供生成评估指标所需的脚本，以及供它检查的产物。</p> <p><a id=\"start-with-evals\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_6\">从评估开始</h2> <p>任务开始前，先定义如何衡量成功。较好的设置通常会结合以下两类检查：</p> <ul> <li><strong>确定性检查：</strong> 脚本可以直接评分的内容，例如是否违反约束，或通过代码计算的确定性指标。</li> <li><strong>大模型评审检查（LLM-as-a-judge）：</strong> 根据评分标准，评估难以精确编码的质量，例如相似度、可读性、实用性或整体质量；可以基于文本或图像输出进行判断。</li> </ul> <p>如果主观质量很重要，就为 Codex 提供一个可调用模型并返回结构化评分的脚本，例如使用 <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/methods/create/index.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Responses API</a>。目的不是替代确定性检查，而是为原本需要人眼评判的部分补充一致的评审方式。</p> <p>评估输出最好采用机器可读格式，每次运行后保存，并便于跨轮比较。</p> <p><strong>提示：</strong> 你可以说明想运行哪些检查，让 Codex 为你生成评估脚本。</p> <p><a id=\"give-codex-a-stopping-rule\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"codex\">为 Codex 设置停止规则</h2> <p>如果提示词只说“持续改进”，却没有说明何时停止，困难任务就容易偏离方向。请明确停止规则。</p> <p>一种实用模式是：</p> <ol> <li>为总评分设定目标。</li> <li>为大模型评审的平均分单独设定目标。</li> <li>要求 Codex 持续推进，直到两项都超过阈值，而不是只满足其中一项。</li> </ol> <p>例如，目标是高质量产物时，可以要求 Codex 持续推进，直到总评分和大模型平均评分都超过 90%。这样任务状态就清晰了：Codex 可以判断是否仍未达标、差距在哪里，以及最近一次改动是否有帮助。</p> <p><a id=\"keep-a-running-log-of-the-loop\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_7\">持续记录循环过程</h2> <p>长期任务中，让 Codex 记录循环过程，比只依赖对话上下文可靠得多。</p> <p>运行记录应包含：</p> <ul> <li>当前最佳评分</li> <li>上一轮迭代改了什么</li> <li>评估表明哪些地方变好了或变差了</li> <li>Codex 下一步准备尝试什么</li> </ul> <p>任务耗时较长时，这一点尤其重要。记录既是任务恢复时的交接点，也是当前这轮工作的自我评估档案。</p> <p><a id=\"inspect-the-artifact-not-just-the-logs\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_8\">检查产物，而不只是日志</h2> <p>对于某些困难任务，仅看代码差异和指标输出还不够。Codex 应直接查看自己生成的产物。</p> <p>如果输出是生成的图像、布局或渲染状态等视觉内容，就让 Codex 直接检查产物。例如，输出是磁盘上的图像时，可以让它将当前结果与此前最佳结果或预定评分标准对照。</p> <p>这样，循环会更有效：</p> <ul> <li>评估脚本报告评分</li> <li>产物展示评分没有发现的问题</li> <li>下一次改动同时以两者为依据</li> </ul> <p>这种组合比不看结果就盲目修改代码有效得多。</p> <p><a id=\"make-every-iteration-explicit\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">明确每一轮迭代</h2> <p>让 Codex 每次都遵循同一套循环：</p> <ol> <li>在当前基线上运行评估。</li> <li>根据评分和产物，找出最主要的失败原因。</li> <li>做一项有针对性的改动来解决该瓶颈。</li> <li>重新运行评估。</li> <li>记录新评分以及改动是否有帮助。</li> <li>持续推进，直到达到阈值。</li> </ol> <p>这种纪律很重要。如果每轮同时改动太多内容，Codex 就无法判断究竟是哪项想法改善了评分。如果跳过记录，任务也会变得难以信任、难以恢复。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"iterate-on-difficult-problems\">Iterate on difficult problems</h1> <p>Use Codex as a scored improvement loop to solve hard tasks.</p> <p>Give Codex an evaluation system, such as scripts and reviewable artifacts, so it can keep improving a hard task until the scores are good enough.</p> <h2 id=\"best-for\">Best for</h2> <ul> <li>Problems where each iteration can be scored, but the best result usually takes many passes</li> <li>Tasks with visual or subjective outputs that need both deterministic checks and an LLM-as-a-judge score</li> <li>Long-running Codex chats where you want progress tracked clearly instead of relying on context</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"starter-prompt\">Starter prompt</h2> <p>Keep Iterating Until the Eval Passes</p> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">I have a difficult task in this workspace and I want you to run it as an eval-driven improvement loop.\n\nBefore changing anything:\n- Read `AGENTS.md`.\n- Find the script or command that scores the current output.\n\nIteration loop:\n- Make one focused improvement at a time.\n- Re-run the eval command after each meaningful change.\n- Log the scores and what changed.\n- Inspect generated artifacts directly. If the output is visual, use `view_image`.\n- Keep going until both the overall score and the LLM average are above 90%.\n\nConstraints:\n- Do not stop at the first acceptable result.\n- Do not revert to an earlier version unless the new result is clearly worse in scores or artifacts.\n- If the eval improves but is still below target, explain the bottleneck and continue.\n\nOutput:\n- current best scores\n- log of major iterations\n- remaining risks or weak spots\n</code></pre> <h2 id=\"related-links\">Related links</h2> <ul> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-94f17e3de1ca7cb3\">Custom instructions with AGENTS.md</a></li> <li><a href=\"#/article/a-0310706ee8617b64\">Codex workflows</a></li> </ul> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <h2 id=\"introduction\">Introduction</h2> <p>Some tasks are easy to verify in one shot: the build passes, the tests go green, and you are done. But there are some optimization problems that are difficult to solve, and need many iterations with a tight evaluation loop. To know which direction to go in, Codex needs to inspect the current output, score it, decide the next change, and repeat until the result is actually good.</p> <p>This type of use case pairs well with a custom UI that lets you inspect progress visually, by having Codex log the outputs and generated artifacts for each iteration. You can watch Codex continue working in the app while the target artifact, model output, or generated asset keeps improving. The key is to give Codex the necessary scripts to generate the evaluation metrics and the artifacts to inspect.</p> <h2 id=\"start-with-evals\">Start with evals</h2> <p>Before the task begins, define how success will be measured. The best setup usually combines:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Deterministic checks:</strong> things the scripts can score directly, such as constraint violations or deterministic metrics computed with code</li> <li><strong>LLM-as-a-judge checks:</strong> rubric-based scores for qualities that are harder to encode exactly, such as resemblance, readability, usefulness, or overall quality - this can rely on text or image outputs</li> </ul> <p>If the subjective part matters, give Codex a script that can call a model for example using the <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses/methods/create/index.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Responses API</a> and return structured scores. The point is not to replace deterministic checks, it's to supplement them with a consistent judge for the part humans would otherwise assess by eye.</p> <p>The loop works best when the eval output is machine-readable, saved after every run, and easy to compare over time.</p> <p><strong>Tip</strong>: Ask Codex to generate the evaluation script for you, describing the checks you want to run.</p> <h2 id=\"give-codex-a-stopping-rule\">Give Codex a stopping rule</h2> <p>Hard tasks often drift because the prompt says “keep improving” without saying when to stop. Make the stopping rule explicit.</p> <p>A practical pattern is:</p> <ol> <li>Set a target for the overall score.</li> <li>Set a separate target for the LLM-judge average.</li> <li>Tell Codex to continue until both are above the threshold, not just one.</li> </ol> <p>For example, if the goal is a high-quality artifact, ask Codex to keep going until both the overall score and the LLM average are above 90%. That makes the task legible: Codex can tell whether it is still below target, where the gap is, and whether the latest change helped.</p> <h2 id=\"keep-a-running-log-of-the-loop\">Keep a running log of the loop</h2> <p>Long-running work is much more reliable when Codex keeps notes about the loop instead of relying only on the chat context.</p> <p>That running log should record:</p> <ul> <li>the current best scores</li> <li>what changed on the last iteration</li> <li>what the eval said got better or worse</li> <li>what Codex plans to try next</li> </ul> <p>This is especially important when the task runs for a long time. The log becomes the handoff point when the task resumes and the self-evaluation record for the current run.</p> <h2 id=\"inspect-the-artifact-not-just-the-logs\">Inspect the artifact, not just the logs</h2> <p>For some difficult tasks, the code diff and metric output are not enough. Codex should look at the artifact it produced.</p> <p>If the output is visual, such as a generated image, layout, or rendered state, let Codex inspect that artifact directly, for example when the output lives on disk as an image and compare the current result to the prior best result or to the intended rubric.</p> <p>This makes the loop stronger:</p> <ul> <li>the eval script reports the score</li> <li>the artifact shows what the score missed</li> <li>the next change is grounded in both</li> </ul> <p>That combination is much more effective than changing code blindly between runs.</p> <h2 id=\"make-every-iteration-explicit\">Make every iteration explicit</h2> <p>Ask Codex to follow the same loop every time:</p> <ol> <li>Run the evals on the current baseline.</li> <li>Identify the biggest failure mode from the scores and artifacts.</li> <li>Make one focused change that addresses that bottleneck.</li> <li>Re-run the evals.</li> <li>Log the new scores and whether the change helped.</li> <li>Continue until the thresholds are met.</li> </ol> <p>This discipline matters. If each iteration changes too many things at once, Codex cannot tell which idea improved the score. If it skips logging, the task becomes hard to trust and hard to resume.</p>"};
